 # Incident log
 
 Newest first. Started 2026-08-10; nothing before that date was written up.
+
+## 2026-09-22 — Half our closed tickets shipped wrong because the requirement sat in a comment posted before work started
+
+> *What happened:* periodic read of the closed-ticket export (12 tickets,
+> 2026-03-02 to 2026-08-01; 9 reopened). In six of them (six of the nine
+> reopens), product or design posted the requirement as a comment one to two
+> days after the ticket was created and
+> before it moved to In Progress: INV-101 (large-account sort), INV-104 (status
+> text for screen readers), INV-107 (first-invoice empty state), INV-110
+> (locale dates), INV-113 (Viewer must not see bank details), INV-116 (phone
+> filter layout). Each time, dev built exactly the acceptance criteria, and the
+> fix was exactly the comment. The six have six different symptoms (sort,
+> a11y, empty state, locale, permissions, mobile). Four carry no shared
+> label, and the `ux` label on 101 and 107 does not group them either.
+> *Why nothing caught it:* the acceptance criteria were the only thing any gate
+> checked. Dev built to them ("alphabetical sort per AC", "four badge colours
+> per AC"). QA caught four (101, 107, 113, 116). On the other two, QA recorded
+> "Matches AC" and "Passes AC", and users found the problem (104, 110). The
+> export can't show whether QA read the comments on the four it caught. The
+> requirement existed, it
+> was correct, and it was posted on time. It was in the place nobody builds
+> from.
+> *The rule:* the description is the requirement. A requirement-bearing
+> comment is copied into the acceptance criteria before the ticket goes to In
+> Progress. A later one is an edit to the criteria, and a comment announces it.
+> Asking people to read comments more carefully is not the fix, because that is
+> exactly what failed six times.
+> *Also in the window, not picked:* two tickets where one word meant two things
+> (INV-102 "hidden", INV-114 "active"). Both were found by users, and a readback
+> would fix them. Symptom labels are empty on 5 of 12 tickets. The export skips
+> INV-103/106/109/112/115, so check whether it is filtered. The retrieval only
+> sees product and design comments in the tracker. Requirements that lived in
+> design frames, chat, or support comments are not counted, so six is a floor.
+> Members were selected by role and date and then read by hand.
+
+```
+Theme: new — requirement lives in a comment; no prior label exists, the log was empty
+Window: `grep -o "\"id\":\"INV-[0-9]*\"" tickets.jsonl` → 12 items
